        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What is a Data Structure?",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A way of organizing and storing data in computer memory. The goal: access, modify and process that data efficiently."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Why Data Structures?",
          "items": [
            "Store data in an organized way",
            "Perform operations efficiently",
            "Cut down time & space complexity",
            "Solve problems the optimized way"
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Built-in Structures",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Type",
            "Ordered / Mutable",
            "Dupes",
            "Access & Use"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "List [1,2,3]",
              "✓ / ✓",
              "Yes",
              "index ✓ — editable collection"
            ],
            [
              "Tuple (1,2,3)",
              "✓ / ✗",
              "Yes",
              "index ✓ — fixed, faster"
            ],
            [
              "Set {1,2,3}",
              "✗ / ✓",
              "No",
              "no index — fast lookup"
            ],
            [
              "Dict {'a':1,'b':2}",
              "✓* / ✓",
              "Keys no",
              "by key — very fast lookup"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "* Sets are unordered — no concept of index. * Dicts keep insertion order since Python 3.7+."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Mutable vs Immutable",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Two Camps",
          "items": [
            "Mutable (can change): list, set, dict",
            "Immutable: tuple, str, int",
            "Also immutable: float, bool, frozenset"
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Quick Memory Trick",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "LIST = Ordered + Mutable + Dupes [10,20,30]",
            "TUPLE = Ordered + Immutable + Dupes (10,20,30)",
            "SET = Unordered + Mutable + Unique {10,20,30}",
            "DICT = Key-Value + Ordered + Unique keys {'a':1}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. When to Use What?",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "List — ordered, you will modify it; eg: cart items",
            "Tuple — data must stay fixed; eg: coordinates (x,y)",
            "Set — unique items + fast membership; eg: usernames",
            "Dict — mapping key to value; eg: user id to name"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Interview Tip",
          "text": "Always pick the right structure — it lifts both readability and efficiency."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Key Takeaway",
          "text": "Right Data Structure = Efficient Code."
        }
      ]
    },
